{
  "id": "BP-DESIGNER-34-sub-process-proof",
  "bp_entry": "BP-DESIGNER-34",
  "title": "A process definition as a sub-process of another: input filtered, output merged, child failure and a missing or upgraded child shown on the parent",
  "description": "T-2844 (owner question: can a process definition be a sub-process of another?). Runs on the platform tenant as the platform administrator with throwaway definitions uat-tmp-subproof-<run_id>-<n>; no platform function, no mail, no provisioner (a child that declares a platform call is refused by the engine, T-2588, so the children are human tasks). 1: parent P calls child C as a SUB_PROCESS with an interface (input order_no, output child_result): the child instance appears, an extra parent variable does not reach it, the child's output is merged into P, P completes, its step log shows the call and its history a SUB_PROCESS_COMPLETED event. 2: the child ends without the output the parent requires: the parent ends in ERROR (EXECUTION_ERROR event), never a silent success; the dead-letter entry, if there is one, is retried. 3: the child definition is removed: a new start of the parent is refused 404 and nothing starts. 4: the child is upgraded while the parent waits before the call: OBSERVED from the code (SubProcess.resolve_child_definition, Definitions.get_active_by_name when the node is entered): the node is late-bound by name, so the parent runs the version of the child that is active when the node is entered, not the one active when the parent started; the step U8 asserts it and the smoke output is the proof. 5: a parent that names an unknown child is saved (the code has no definition-time check of the child name); a start is refused 404. Whether save or activate should refuse it with a code is a product question, not asserted here.",
